{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 46, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades a powerful and ambitious ruler who navigated complex political landscapes and faced constant threats from Muslim forces and rival Crusader states. His reign was marked by military campaigns diplomatic maneuvering and extensive building projects solidifying Tripoli's position as a key Crusader stronghold.\n\n*Early Life and Accession* Raymond III was born around 1120 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli. He inherited the county upon his father's death in 1154. His early years were spent consolidating his authority within Tripoli dealing with internal disputes and strengthening the county's defenses. He faced challenges from rebellious vassals and neighboring Muslim emirs. He inherited a county that was economically prosperous due to its strategic location on the Mediterranean trade routes. His father had fostered trade and encouraged the growth of Tripoli as a major commercial center. Raymond III continued this policy promoting commerce and attracting merchants from across Europe and the Middle East.\n\nRaymond III's reign was characterized by extensive military activity. He engaged in numerous campaigns against the Ayyubid dynasty rulers of Egypt and Syria a major power in the region. These campaigns often involved alliances with other Crusader states like the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He participated in several major Crusades including the Seventh Crusade and the Eighth Crusade although his involvement in the latter was limited. He also faced constant threats from Muslim forces in the Levant and had to maintain a strong military presence to defend Tripoli's borders.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances* Raymond III was a skilled diplomat who understood the importance of alliances in maintaining power. He forged relationships with various rulers including the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II. These alliances provided him with military support and political leverage. He skillfully played rival Crusader states against each other preventing any one state from becoming too dominant. He also engaged in diplomatic negotiations with Muslim rulers seeking to secure truces and maintain peaceful relations whenever possible. His diplomatic efforts were crucial in preserving Tripoli's independence and prosperity.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III died in 1199 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV. His reign is generally viewed as a period of stability and prosperity for the County of Tripoli. He successfully defended Tripoli against numerous threats and strengthened the county's economic and military power. His building projects including the construction of castles and fortifications further enhanced Tripoli's defenses. He left behind a well-defended and prosperous county that continued to play a significant role in the Crusader states for many years after his death. He is remembered as one of the most effective and influential rulers of Tripoli during the Crusades. His strategic acumen and diplomatic skills allowed him to navigate the treacherous political landscape of the Middle East and maintain Tripoli's independence for a considerable period.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2371193766593933}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.20648594200611115}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.054002758115530014}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00023135847004596144}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.5987286790041253e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.583032